<description>
No fuzz target feeds V2Transport bytes that an honest peer would not send past the v2 key exchange. I add p2p_transport_v2_receive. It knows the transport's keys, builds the input from arbitrary bytes and from parts of a valid handshake (the peer key, garbage, the terminator, encrypted packets that may be corrupted, and runs of empty decoys that force a rekey), and feeds it in pieces of arbitrary size. Once the transport reports v2, it checks that its session id matches the peer's.

It reaches the v2 receive states and their failures, except the fallback to v1 and the wrong-magic disconnect, which need a v1 `version` header at the start of the stream; `p2p_transport_bidirectional_v1v2` reaches the first. 15 minutes with libFuzzer, ASan and UBSan found no crash.
</description>

<commits>
b2636036e6 fuzz: Add a target that feeds arbitrary bytes to V2Transport

No fuzz target feeds V2Transport bytes that an honest peer would not
send past the v2 key exchange.

Add p2p_transport_v2_receive. It knows the keys of the transport it
tests, and builds a byte stream from arbitrary bytes and from the parts
of a valid handshake: the peer key, garbage, the garbage terminator,
encrypted packets, which may be corrupted, and runs of empty decoys,
which make the transport rekey. The stream is received in pieces of
arbitrary size.
This reaches the v2 receive states and their failures, except two
that need the 16-byte v1 prefix: the fallback to v1, which
p2p_transport_bidirectional_v1v2 reaches, and the disconnect of a v1
peer with the wrong magic. Once the transport reports v2, its session
id must match the peer's.
</commits>

